const chapters = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "how-it-works", label: "How the night works" },
  { id: "what-happened", label: "What happened" },
  {
    id: "under-the-hood",
    label: "Under the hood",
    children: [
      { id: "payments", label: "Payment that's safe to retry" },
      { id: "the-door", label: "A door with no signal" },
      { id: "transfers", label: "Uploads that survive hotel wifi" },
      { id: "live-rooms", label: "Live rooms" },
      { id: "scoring", label: "Scoring a guess" },
      { id: "writing-it-down", label: "Writing it down" },
    ],
  },
  { id: "lessons", label: "What I learned" },
];
---

<StoryLayout
  title="Milk & Henny"
  description="One site that runs a whole party: tickets, the door, games on the TV and the photos afterwards, and how it's kept from breaking."
  chapters={chapters}
  groups={[{ id: "night", threads: steps }]}
>
  <StoryChapter id="problem" title="The problem">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>I host parties in London. A night like that is a lot of small jobs: selling tickets, getting people through the door, getting strangers talking, and sending everyone the photos afterwards.</p>
      <p>I could have glued that together from separate tools. Instead one site does all of it, and I'm the one running it on the night. When something breaks, I'm the person standing at the door.</p>
      <p>That's the brief I hold it to: the night must not break. Not when two payment messages race, not when the door loses signal, not when someone's phone drops out of a game.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="how-it-works" title="How the night works" group="night">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>From the ticket to the photos. Public pages are recorded on the live site; the door, the host's tools and the guest's own screens on a local build with made-up guests.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseJourney project="milk-and-henny" steps={steps} />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="what-happened" title="What happened">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>It's real, and it's small. A read-only production query in September 2026 counted 119 tickets across three events, and 908 emails sent through the outbox. That's enough to find the real failure modes. It isn't scale, and I won't pretend it is.</p>
      <p>The games are built and tested in software, but playtests with real groups are still to come. The playtest log is blunt about it: empty observations are pending, not passes.</p>
      <p>The exception is Hot & Cold's daily word, which people did play. A backup from 26 September 2026 holds 122 finished games of 30 daily words, played between 29 August and 25 September. 88 found the word, with a median of 26.5 guesses. These are games, one per browser per word, not people, and some are mine.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="under-the-hood" title="Under the hood" part>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>For engineers: where money, doors and phones meet the software, and how each one is kept honest.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="payments" title="Payment that's safe to retry" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>When a guest pays, two things happen: Stripe sends a webhook, and the guest lands on a success page. Either can arrive first, either can arrive twice, and either can die halfway through.</p>
      <p>Both call the same fulfilment, and it only goes ahead if it can claim the order with one conditional update, from pending to fulfilling. A claim that has been stuck for more than two minutes can be taken over, so a crashed attempt never strands a ticket.</p>
      <p>If the last tickets sell out between paying and fulfilling, the guest gets a refund automatically. The comment in the code says it better than I can: “Give the money back rather than hold it for an event they cannot attend.”</p>
      <p>Refunds, failed refunds and disputes come back through the same webhook. Win a dispute and the voided tickets are restored.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="the-door" title="A door with no signal" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Online, check-in claims an unused ticket inside a Postgres transaction, so if two scanners submit the same ticket only one wins.</p>
      <p>Offline, the scanner works from a list of shortened hashes rather than the tickets themselves, because, in the code's words, “a device left in a taxi cannot be turned into a ticket forgery kit.” The offline queue is a pure function, capped at 5,000 admissions a shift.</p>
      <p>It catches a duplicate scan on the same phone. Two phones offline at the same time can't settle a duplicate between them, so those still need reconciling afterwards, and the design says so instead of pretending otherwise.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="transfers" title="Uploads that survive hotel wifi" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The code's own example: someone empties a phone after a wedding, gigabytes over hotel wifi. So the browser uploads straight to storage on signed links that last six hours, and the server reserves the transfer's space before the first byte moves. After a refresh, the page resends only the files that hadn't finished.</p>
      <p>Adding files to a live transfer reserves their count and bytes under a lock on the transfer, so two batches at once can't overbook it or reuse a filename. The owner link carries a delete token. The database keeps only its hash, plus an encrypted copy so an interrupted upload can still hand it back.</p>
      <p>Originals are kept exactly as sent. Previews come from a separate media worker. Each attempt writes to its own key, and only the attempt that still holds the job publishes, so a slow worker can't overwrite a newer result. Before a release, an audit checks every live file is in storage at the right size.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="live-rooms" title="Live rooms" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Phones drop out of games all the time: a lock screen, a tunnel, a flat battery. Each room changes state through a pure reducer, every action carries an ID, and the engine remembers which IDs it has already applied. A phone that resends after reconnecting gets the earlier result replayed, not applied twice.</p>
      <p>In Redis, a room is guarded by a lock that expires after 15 seconds and can only be released by whoever holds it, so a lock that lapsed and passed to someone else is never pulled from under them.</p>
      <p>The Postgres version locks the room row, stores a receipt for each action with the room's revision number, and writes the game result to an outbox in the same transaction.</p>
    </div>
    <Clip project="milk-and-henny" beat="rejoin" caption="Rejoining a room by its code in Imposter. The code is typed, never sent." />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="scoring" title="Scoring a guess" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Hot & Cold runs no model when you guess. For each target, a build step ranks all 32,508 words in its dictionary by meaning, using a small open embedding model and Open English WordNet. The ranks ship as packed tables of 16-bit numbers, so a guess is a lookup. It's instant, and every phone gets the same answer.</p>
      <p>Every result records the judging version that scored it. Four days after launch I changed the judging, the old scores stopped being comparable, and I wiped the board rather than rank games scored by different rules. Results are now keyed by game and version, and the community numbers only count games judged the same way.</p>
      <p>The browser counts a daily game, so the server bounds it. The word and version come from the server, heat counts can't exceed guesses, each game counts once, and one address can post 30 an hour. In a room the server scores every guess, checks whose turn it is, and keeps the word hidden until the round ends.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="writing-it-down" title="Writing it down" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Emails and game results leave through an outbox, written in the same transaction as the change that caused them. If the process dies, the work is still there to finish.</p>
      <p>When things go wrong they get a postmortem. One idle media worker quietly used 450,061 of a 500,000-command free Redis allowance. Another bug read the guest list once a second, for no reason at all.</p>
      <p>Before an event there's an event-night readiness ledger. Each check is graded by its evidence: seen in a browser, checked at the API and database, covered by a test, checked physically on the night, or still open. At its last count it recorded 1,448 passing tests and 12 browser journeys.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="lessons" title="What I learned">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Hosting is the best test I know. Can someone understand this screen while a queue is forming? Can the room keep playing when one phone drops?</p>
      <p>That makes the recovery path part of the main design. The failed scan, the double payment message and the reconnecting phone aren't edge cases on a party night. They're what a normal night looks like.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>
</StoryLayout>
